FreeBSD-EN-26:26.hastd                                          Errata Notice
                                                          The FreeBSD Project

Topic:          Multiple issues in hastd(8)

Category:       core
Module:         hastd
Announced:      2026-10-14
Credits:        Alessandro Sagratini, Martin Vidovic, Glen Barber
Affects:        All supported versions of FreeBSD.
Corrected:      2026-09-20 17:07:37 UTC (stable/15, 15.1-STABLE)
                2026-10-14 16:04:32 UTC (releng/15.1, 15.1-RELEASE-p5)
                2026-09-20 17:07:56 UTC (stable/14, 14.5-STABLE)
                2026-10-14 16:04:08 UTC (releng/14.5, 14.5-RELEASE-p2)
                2026-10-14 16:03:50 UTC (releng/14.4, 14.4-RELEASE-p11)

For general information regarding FreeBSD Errata Notices and Security
Advisories, including descriptions of the fields above, security
branches, and the following sections, please visit
<URL:https://security.FreeBSD.org/>.

I.   Background

hastd(8) allows transparent replication of a block of storage (the
resource) between a pair of systems connected over a TCP/IP network.
At any given time, only one system (the primary node) can access the
resource.  Writes performed on the primary node are continuously
transmitted to the other system (the secondary node).

hastctl(8) is a command-line program used to control a hastd(8)
instance on the same machine.

II.  Problem Description

All communication between hastd nodes, between hastd and hastctl, and
between the main hastd process and its child processes relies on a
shared set of networking routines and an early implementation of
name-value pair lists similar to but distinct from libnv(3), which was
a later development.

1. Control messages between hastd and hastctl can be empty, consisting
   solely of a header with no payload.  However, after receiving the
   header, the function used to receive control messages asserts that
   buffer space for the payload was successfully allocated.  When
   there is no payload, this assertion fails.

2. The nvpair implementation used by hastd and hastctl pads names and
   values out to multiples of eight bytes, but does not initialize the
   padding.  As a result, small amounts of data from reused heap
   allocations can find their way into that padding.

3. The main hastd process uses local domain sockets to communicate
   with its workers.  When the main hastd process hands off an
   incoming connection to a worker, it first sends a variable-length
   string indicating the type of socket.  The child receives this
   message using a function which does not allow short reads.  This
   previously worked due to a bug in the local domain socket code
   which forced a short read if a normal message was followed by one
   that passed a descriptor.

III. Impact

1. Depending on the exact configuration, certain hastctl commands may
   result in an empty response from hastd, causing hastctl to print an
   error message and abort.  This does not affect the operation of
   hastd itself, but may make it difficult to inquire into its status
   or change its mode of operation.

2. The most obvious symptom of the padding issue is that small amounts
   of text from the configuration file end up embedded in the metadata
   at the start of the backing store for a HAST resource, because the
   memory that was first used to read the configuration is later
   reused to assemble the metadata before writing it out.

   Small amounts of hastd memory, potentially including data
   previously written to the resource, may also end up embedded in
   network messages passed between hastd nodes.

3. As a pair of HAST nodes transition from initialization to
   operation, the primary node will attempt to establish a network
   connection to the secondary node.  The secondary node accepts the
   connection, then hands the socket off to a worker child.  The
   worker child then hangs forever trying to read the socket type,
   preventing the node from transitioning to secondary.  The pair
   remains forever in degraded mode, where the primary is able to
   operate normally but no replication to the secondary occurs.

IV.  Workaround

No workaround is available, but systems that do not use hastd(8) are not
affected.

V.   Solution

Upgrade your system to a supported FreeBSD stable or release / security
branch (releng) dated after the correction date.  Restart hastd(8), or
reboot the system.

Perform one of the following:

1) To update your system installed from base system packages:

Systems running a 15.0-RELEASE or later version of FreeBSD on the amd64 or
arm64 platforms, which were installed using base system packages, can be
updated via the pkg(8) utility:

# pkg upgrade -r FreeBSD-base

2) To update your system installed from binary distribution sets:

Systems running a RELEASE version of FreeBSD on the amd64 or arm64 platforms
which were not installed using base system packages can be updated via the
freebsd-update(8) utility:

# freebsd-update fetch
# freebsd-update install

3) To update your system via a source code patch:

The following patches have been verified to apply to the applicable
FreeBSD release branches.

a) Download the relevant patch from the location below, and verify the
detached PGP signature using your PGP utility.

# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/EN-26:26/hastd.patch
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/EN-26:26/hastd.patch.asc
# gpg --verify hastd.patch.asc

b) Apply the patch.  Execute the following commands as root:

# cd /usr/src
# patch -E -p0 < /path/to/patch

c) Recompile the operating system using buildworld and installworld as
described in <URL:https://www.FreeBSD.org/handbook/makeworld.html>.

Restart hastd(8), or reboot the system.

VI.  Correction details

This issue is corrected as of the corresponding Git commit hash in the
following stable and release branches:

Branch/path                             Hash                     Revision
